[1;33mXREF(1)[0m                    [1;33mUNIX Programmer's Manual[0m                     [1;33mXREF(1)[0m

[1;33mNAME[0m
     xref - what is a cross-reference and what only looks like one

[1;33mDESCRIPTION[0m
     See [3;35mopen[0m(2) and the discussion in [3;35mls[0m(1). A subsection letter is part of
     it, so [3;35mfsck[0m(1m) and stdio.3s and [3;35mexp[0m(3m) are references too, and a name
     may begin with an underscore: [3;35m_exit[0m(2).

     The name must start at a word boundary, so [3;35msprintf[0m(3) is one reference and
     not two: the printf inside it does not match on its own.

     A reference must carry no markup, and these are therefore not references:
     [1;32mopen(2)[0m is a bold run, [3;36mls(1)[0m is an italic one, and `[4mprintf(3)[0m' is a
     literal one.

     Nor is anything whose shape is wrong: open(9) has no such section, open
     (2) has a space in it, open(2 has no close, and 9open(2) does not begin
     with a letter.

[1;33mSEE ALSO[0m
     [3;35mmalloc[0m(3), [3;35mfree[0m(3), [3;35mrealloc[0m(3), [3;35mcalloc[0m(3)
